| Area | Current behavior / scaling implication |
|---|---|
| **JWT signing key** | All A instances must use the same `AUTH_JWT_KEY`; independently generated local keys would cause intermittent authentication failures. |
| **Endpoint encryption key** | Use the same `SIGNING_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY` wherever these endpoint secrets are encrypted/decrypted. |
| **Internal service token** | A instances and B must agree on `SERVICE_INTERNAL_TOKEN`. |
| **PostgreSQL / RabbitMQ** | All replicas must connect to the same logical database and broker topology. |
| **Outbox claiming** | `SKIP LOCKED` and claim leases coordinate publishers across instances. Lease expiry can permit duplicate publishing. |
| **JWT / refresh state** | JWT verification is stateless; refresh families are stored in shared PostgreSQL. No sticky sessions are needed for these. |
| **Application lock** | Ingestion/replay for the **same `app_id` serialize** on its application row. More instances won’t remove that per-application bottleneck. |
| **Reconciliation** | The shared cursor lock allows one reconciliation page at a time. Safe across replicas, but this work does not scale proportionally. |
| **Shared infrastructure capacity** | More instances add database connections, queries, and broker consumers; PostgreSQL/RabbitMQ can become the bottleneck. |

